<%!-- Step 2 of a rename (issue #1086): prove it is you.

One page, one job. The top card states plainly that the rename has NOT happened
yet and what it will do; the bottom card holds every way this member can confirm
it, strongest first. A member with only an email address and only one of those
never sees a choice at all - the PIN is already in their inbox by the time this
renders, so the page reads as "type the number we sent you".

The top card is written against one specific misreading. It used to open with an
h1 calling the pending handle "your new username" and a diff whose OLD half was
`line-through`, under a heading "What will change" - three signals that all say
"done", on a page whose entire point is that nothing is done. Stefan read his own
rename as finished and the PIN as paperwork. So: the first thing in the card is
the flat statement that nothing changed and which handle he still holds, the two
states are LABELLED ("Now" / "After you confirm") instead of being a diff the
reader has to interpret, and no strikethrough appears anywhere - a struck-through
handle is the single most misleading pixel this page can render. --%>
<.settings_shell
  user={@user}
  active={:username}
  title={gettext("Change your username?")}
  crumbs={[
    {gettext("Settings"), ~p"/settings"},
    {gettext("Username"), ~p"/settings/username"},
    gettext("Confirm")
  ]}
>
  <div class="space-y-6">
    <.card>
      <%!-- `role="status"` so a screen reader is told the state, not just shown
      it. Deliberately brand tint, not the amber reserved for moderation: this is
      a state, not a warning. Its headline is a step LARGER than the body text
      around it, because the failure mode being fixed is "did not read it". --%>
      <div
        id="rename-pending-notice"
        role="status"
        class="rounded-xl bg-brand-50 p-4 ring-1 ring-brand-100 dark:bg-brand-900/30 dark:ring-brand-800"
      >
        <p class="text-base font-semibold text-brand-800 dark:text-brand-100">
          {gettext("Nothing has changed yet.")}
        </p>
        <p class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed text-brand-800 dark:text-brand-100">
          {pending_notice_line(@options, @user.username)}
        </p>
      </div>

      <.section_title class="mt-6">{gettext("Before and after")}</.section_title>
      <%!-- Two labelled cells, not a diff: each states its own time and status,
      so neither has to be inferred from a glyph. Stacks on a phone. --%>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="rounded-xl bg-slate-50 p-3 ring-1 ring-slate-200 dark:bg-slate-800/60 dark:ring-slate-700">
          <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
            {gettext("Now")}
          </p>
          <p class="mt-1 font-mono text-sm font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">
            @{@user.username}
          </p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
            {gettext("Your profile address, unchanged.")}
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-xl bg-slate-50 p-3 ring-1 ring-slate-200 dark:bg-slate-800/60 dark:ring-slate-700">
          <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
            {gettext("After you confirm")}
          </p>
          <%!-- Same slate as the current handle on purpose. Brand blue here read
          as a link (the real one, "Pick a different username", sits just below in
          that colour) and, worse, tinted the pending name as the live one. The
          two cells carry equal weight; their labels do the distinguishing. --%>
          <p
            id="pending-username"
            class="mt-1 font-mono text-sm font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100"
          >
            @{@new_username}
          </p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
            {gettext("Not yours yet.")}
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
        {gettext(
          "Once you confirm, your current profile address stops working, and anyone can claim the handle you leave behind. Mentions of your old handle in posts are rewritten to the new one."
        )}
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm">
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/settings/username"}
          class="font-semibold text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-700"
        >
          {gettext("Pick a different username")}
        </.link>
      </p>
    </.card>

    <.card>
      <.section_title>{gettext("Confirm it is you")}</.section_title>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
        {confirm_intro_line(@options)}
      </p>

      <%!-- "We sent a PIN to <address>" is the INSTRUCTION on this card - go and
      open that inbox - so once a PIN is really in flight it is a notice above
      the field, not a caption under it. It was the muted `.editform__hint`
      first, then semibold in the same spot, and Stefan reported it as buried
      both times: weight alone could not fix it, because anything below the input
      reads as a footnote you skip on your way to the button. Above the field it
      is read before typing, and the envelope plus the panel give it a shape the
      eye catches. Same brand tint as the "Nothing has changed yet" notice on
      purpose - both are "here is the state of things", one vocabulary. --%>
      <div
        :if={@pin_email}
        id="pin-sent-notice"
        role="status"
        class="mt-4 flex items-start gap-3 rounded-xl bg-brand-50 p-4 ring-1 ring-brand-100 dark:bg-brand-900/30 dark:ring-brand-800"
      >
        <svg
          xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
          fill="none"
          viewBox="0 0 24 24"
          stroke-width="1.5"
          stroke="currentColor"
          aria-hidden="true"
          class="mt-0.5 h-6 w-6 shrink-0 text-brand-700 dark:text-brand-100"
        >
          <path
            stroke-linecap="round"
            stroke-linejoin="round"
            d="M21.75 6.75v10.5a2.25 2.25 0 0 1-2.25 2.25h-15a2.25 2.25 0 0 1-2.25-2.25V6.75m19.5 0A2.25 2.25 0 0 0 19.5 4.5h-15a2.25 2.25 0 0 0-2.25 2.25m19.5 0v.243a2.25 2.25 0 0 1-1.07 1.916l-7.5 4.615a2.25 2.25 0 0 1-2.36 0L3.32 8.91a2.25 2.25 0 0 1-1.07-1.916V6.75"
          />
        </svg>
        <p class="text-base font-semibold leading-relaxed text-brand-800 dark:text-brand-100">
          {code_field_hint(@options, @pin_email)}
        </p>
      </div>

      <%!-- The passkey path, for members who enrolled one. Hidden until app.js
      confirms the browser supports WebAuthn; the ceremony only stamps the
      session, then submits the plain form below it. --%>
      <div :if={@options.passkey?} id="passkey-confirm" hidden class="mt-4">
        <button
          type="button"
          data-webauthn-confirm
          data-challenge-url={~p"/settings/username/passkey/challenge"}
          data-verify-url={~p"/settings/username/passkey"}
          data-submit-form="username-passkey-form"
          data-error-generic={gettext("That passkey could not be verified. Please try again.")}
          class="rounded-lg bg-brand-600 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-semibold text-white hover:bg-brand-700"
        >
          {gettext("Confirm with your passkey")}
        </button>
        <p data-webauthn-error hidden class="mt-2 text-sm text-red-600 dark:text-red-400"></p>

        <.form
          :let={_f}
          for={%{}}
          as={:username_confirmation}
          action={~p"/settings/username/confirm"}
          method="post"
          id="username-passkey-form"
          class="hidden"
        >
          <button type="submit" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true">
            {gettext("Confirm")}
          </button>
        </.form>
      </div>

      <p
        :if={@options.passkey?}
        data-webauthn-unsupported
        hidden
        class="mt-4 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400"
      >
        {gettext("This browser does not support passkeys. Use a code below instead.")}
      </p>

      <%!-- The email-PIN sender sits ABOVE the code field exactly when the member
      has nothing to type yet (no PIN in flight, no authenticator app): there,
      getting a code is the first step, and burying that button under the submit
      leaves them staring at an empty field. In every other state they already
      have a code, so it drops below as the quiet "send it again". --%>
      <.pin_sender
        :if={pin_sender_first?(@options, @pin_email)}
        options={@options}
        pin_email={@pin_email}
        class="mt-4"
      />

      <%!-- The code field. One field for every kind of code the member has, the
      same way the login PIN field accepts an authenticator code (issue #912),
      so nobody has to work out which box their code belongs in. --%>
      <.form
        :let={f}
        for={%{}}
        as={:username_confirmation}
        action={~p"/settings/username/confirm"}
        method="post"
        id="username-confirm-form"
        class="editform mt-6"
      >
        <div class="editform__field">
          <%= label(f, :code, code_field_label(@options)) %>
          <%= text_input(f, :code,
            autofocus: true,
            autocomplete: "one-time-code",
            inputmode: (@options.list_codes? && "text") || "numeric",
            spellcheck: "false",
            autocapitalize: "none",
            placeholder: gettext("Enter your code")
          ) %>
          <%!-- Only while nothing has been mailed: then this line genuinely is a
          hint about what the field accepts. The moment a PIN names a real
          address it moves up into `#pin-sent-notice` above the field, so it is
          never rendered twice. --%>
          <p :if={!@pin_email} class="editform__hint">
            {code_field_hint(@options, @pin_email)}
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="editform__actions">
          <%= link(gettext("Cancel"), to: ~p"/settings/username", class: "button button--cancel") %>
          <%= submit(gettext("Change my username"), class: "button") %>
        </div>
      </.form>

      <.pin_sender
        :if={!pin_sender_first?(@options, @pin_email)}
        options={@options}
        pin_email={@pin_email}
        class="mt-6 border-t border-slate-200 pt-4 dark:border-slate-800"
      />
    </.card>
  </div>
</.settings_shell>
